# RULES: OPERATIONAL

## Gates per boundary

Fill in during synthesis with the real framework and tool commands of the project. Each gate defines: ID, command, trigger, and expected result.

| ID | Command | Trigger | Expected result |
|---|---|---|---|
| `analyze` | <framework static analysis, e.g. analyzer, linter, typecheck> | any code diff | zero new/in-scope issues |
| `validate` | <project structural validator, when present> | diff in rules/structure | index/graph intact |
| `diff-check` | `git diff HEAD --check` | tracked diff | zero whitespace/patch errors |

- The placeholders above must be replaced during synthesis with the real commands of the repository (e.g. `flutter analyze`, `tsc`, `eslint`, `melos run ...`).
- Structural gates do not replace static analysis.
- Docs/config-only diffs with no code do not trigger analysis gates.

## Self-containment

- Engineering norms must live in `AGENTS.md` or `project-rules/`.
- A structural rule/reference/contract never references an external file to complete a decision; a spec/PRD may be consulted as a product requirement, never as a normative dependency.
- Every rule, reference, and contract must be reachable from at least one index.

## Tests

- Unit/integration/e2e tests are only created/executed when the user explicitly asks.
- Analyzers and structural validators are gates, not tests for this restriction.
- A test that merely crystallizes a bad implementation (fragile design, undue coupling, workaround) is an anti-pattern; prefer a structural invariant at the source.

## Failures and baseline

- Fix failures you introduced or inside the boundary.
- A pre-existing external failure does not authorize adjacent refactoring: record command/evidence, separate the baseline, and classify the result.
- Never declare a gate green when the command failed, even if the cause is the baseline.

## Plans/handoffs

- When the user asks for a plan/handoff, propagate the applicable gates into the artifact.
- Do not copy gates without a trigger; each item states command, boundary, and acceptance criterion.

## Closeout

- Report applied rules, executed commands, results, and pendings.
- Classification: `ready`, `degraded-but-usable`, or `needs-followup`.
- Commit/push only on explicit request.
